@nsobid-815A2C19D176789688FF33EA875C26C5

# Hyrule Warriors: Definitive Edition 1.0.1 - 60 FPS cutscenes

@flag print_values
@flag offset_shift 0x100

// Runs real-time (in-engine) cutscenes at 60 FPS instead of 30 FPS, at
// normal speed.
//
// The game has no present-interval switch for these scenes. Its main loop is
// paced by a sleep limiter that waits "step" 1/59.83 s ticks per update, and
// one setter writes the step together with the matching frames-per-second
// int and float that the rest of the engine reads. Gameplay runs with step 1.
// Starting an event saves the current step and calls the setter with 2; the
// end of the event restores the saved value.
//
// This makes the event start request step 1 instead. Event animation, script
// timers and the rest of the scene advance by the step each update, so they
// take twice as many updates of half the size and play at the same speed.
// Nothing else changes: the step is restored on exit exactly as stock.
//
// Cutscenes that play inside a battle scene also pass through the battle
// update, which overwrites the event playback rate with a constant 1.0 every
// update. At stock that equals the event path's own value (step 2 x 0.5), but
// at step 1 it would double their speed, so the constant becomes 0.5. The
// rate is only read while an event is playing.
//
// Pre-rendered movies are unaffected; they play through a separate player
// that keeps its own 30 FPS present interval.

@enabled
00252FA4 20008052   // MOVZ W0,#1 = step 1 (was ORR W0,WZR,#2 = step 2 on event start)
00172FA4 09E0A752   // MOVZ W9,#0x3F00,LSL#16 = 0.5f event rate (was 1.0f)
@stop

// Offsets reverse-engineered against Hyrule Warriors: Definitive Edition 1.0.1
// (Koei Tecmo KTGL, NVN). The retail build is symbol-stripped; every site was
// located by structure and byte-verified against the IDB before being written.
//
// https://github.com/RAMDRAGONS/switch-patches
